review:
New York Mysteries: High Voltage is yet another gorgeous crime investigating game featuring Laura James, your favorite 1920s journalist. This time, Laura has to face a supernatural encounter with a murder convict turned into a high voltage entity by his own father. As such let me point out the pros and cons that I've found throughout the game.

PROS:
- Engaging story (Madonna - Die Another Day vibe!); 
- Beautiful backgrounds;
- Repetitive, yet spot on music;
- Easy to learn mechanisc;
- Good voice-acting;
- Various mini-games;
- The Hidden Objects are not organized as an irrelevant list, but you collect objects in order to use them;
- Bonus chapter included (you get to play as the murderer's father!);
- The Bonus chapter has probably the best background images in the game;
- The Slides collectible let you play 20 mini-games apart from the ones in the game;
- The Fast Travelling Map shows the collectibles (easier to collect them) (hint button won't help);

CONS:
- Too many collectibles;
- No trading cards.

In conclusion, I heartly recommend this game to detective crime investigating casual game fans! As I have played it, it seems that this New York Mystery game comes second. So, if you want to know where to start and end the New York Mystery series, go with this sequence: 1.) New York Mysteries: Secrets of the Mafia, 2.) New York Mysteries: High Voltage and 3.) New York Mysteries: The Lantern of Souls (my current favorite). Try get them when there are special price offers on the market. You won't regret.
